+**The x86-64 late vectorizer has a "twin"** (`vectorize_function_late`) in
+addition to the shared dispatch entry. I had wrapped the first in
+`LateMinmaxOnlyScope` and not the second — and the twin is exactly the pass that
+reaches the guarded sums `if_convert` materialises, so the restriction was
+decorative. `zlib_ng_adler32` at `-O2 -march=x86-64-v3`: **312 instructions /
+74 memory refs / 49 ymm refs / a 584-byte frame** unscoped, versus **259 / 57 /
+24 / none** scoped and in #686. Every vector op count was exactly 2×: the twin
+re-vectorised the whole loop into a second, spilled copy (175 extra asm lines).
+Isolation was clean — `CCC_DISABLE_PASSES=latevec` on my binary reproduced
+#686's output exactly. Output was correct throughout (`8c331ae0`, and `b1bf1275`
+at 10×), so this is bloat, not a miscompile; **runtime is neutral within ±1 %**
+(10× scale, min-of-7: 0.2765 / 0.2770 / 0.2741 s vs gcc), and I am not claiming
+a speedup. Survey gap for that function went 239 → 204 vs `icc=73`.
